<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Inverse of Polynomials — inverse of a restricted quadratic</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Let \( f(x) = {@fx@} \) for \( x \geq {@h@} \). The domain is restricted so that \(f\) is one-to-one, and so has an inverse.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
  <li><p>Find \( f^{-1}(x) \).</p>
    <div>\( f^{-1}(x) = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
  <li><p>Complete the domain of \(f^{-1}\).</p>
    <div>\(x \geq\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>
<p>Type \(\sqrt{x}\) as <code>sqrt(x)</code>.</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A quadratic is not one-to-one on all of \(\mathbb{R}\), but on \(x \geq {@h@}\) (to the right of its vertex \(({@h@}, {@k@})\)) it is increasing, so it has an <strong>inverse</strong>. Swap \(x\) and \(y\) in \(y = {@fy0@}\) and solve for \(y\):</p>
\[
\begin{align*}
x &= {@fy@}\\
{@x-k@} &= {@(y-h)^2@}\\
\pm\sqrt{ {@x-k@} } &= {@y-h@}\\
y &= {@h@} \pm \sqrt{ {@x-k@} }
\end{align*}
\]
<p>Taking the square root gives two possible branches. The <strong>domain of \(f\) becomes the range of \(f^{-1}\)</strong>: the original inputs satisfy \(x \geq {@h@}\), so the outputs of \(f^{-1}\) must satisfy \(y \geq {@h@}\). That needs the \(+\) branch:</p>
\[ f^{-1}(x) = {@ta1@}. \]
<p>Likewise the <strong>range of \(f\) becomes the domain of \(f^{-1}\)</strong>. On \(x \geq {@h@}\) the smallest value of \(f\) is \(f({@h@}) = {@k@}\), so the range of \(f\) is \(y \geq {@k@}\) and the domain of \(f^{-1}\) is \(x \geq {@k@}\). This is also where \(\sqrt{ {@x-k@} }\) is defined.</p>
<p>The graph of \(f^{-1}\) is the reflection of the graph of \(f\) in the line \(y = x\). The vertex \(({@h@}, {@k@})\) reflects to the end point \(({@k@}, {@h@})\).</p>
<div style="text-align:center;" role="img" aria-label="The half-parabola y = f(x) for x at least h, its inverse (a square-root curve), and the dashed mirror line y = x.">
[[jsxgraph width="320px" height="320px"]]
(function() {
  var lo = {#bmin#}, hi = {#bmax#};
  var b = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {
    boundingbox: [lo, hi, hi, lo], axis: true, grid: false,
    showNavigation: false, showCopyright: false
  });
  var h = {#h#}, k = {#k#};
  b.create('functiongraph', [function(x){ return (x-h)*(x-h) + k; }, h, h + 4], {strokeColor:'#003399', strokeWidth:2});
  b.create('functiongraph', [function(x){ return h + Math.sqrt(x - k); }, k, k + 16], {strokeColor:'#cc0000', strokeWidth:2});
  b.create('functiongraph', [function(x){ return x; }], {strokeColor:'#666666', dash:2, strokeWidth:1});
  b.create('point', [h, k], {size:3, color:'#003399', fixed:true, name:'(' + h + ', ' + k + ')', label:{offset:[8,-14]}});
  b.create('point', [k, h], {size:3, color:'#cc0000', fixed:true, name:'(' + k + ', ' + h + ')', label:{offset:[8,10]}});
})();
[[/jsxgraph]]
</div>
<p style="text-align:center;"><span style="color:#003399;">Blue: \(y=f(x)\)</span>; <span style="color:#cc0000;">red: \(y=f^{-1}(x)\)</span>; dashed: \(y=x\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
